<worksheet xml:id="sec-quiz-one-proportion-ht-att-3">
  <title>Quiz: One-Proportion Hypothesis Tests Attempt 3</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>Name: <fillin characters="45"/></p>

    <assemblage>
      <p><em>One-proportion hypothesis test formulas and R commands</em></p>
      <p>
        <m>\widehat{p}=\dfrac{x}{n}</m>
        <m>\qquad</m>
        <m>z=\dfrac{\widehat{p}-p_0}{\sqrt{\dfrac{p_0(1-p_0)}{n}}}</m>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>P(Z\le z)=\texttt{pnorm(z)}</m>
        <m>\qquad</m>
        <m>P(Z&gt;z)=\texttt{pnorm(z,lower.tail=FALSE)}</m>
      </p>
      <p>
        Two-tailed p-value: <m>2\cdot\texttt{pnorm(-abs(z))}</m>.
        For critical values, <c>qnorm(q)</c> returns the standard normal
        value with cumulative probability <m>q</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Here <m>p_0</m> is the null-hypothesis reference proportion.
        Normal-approximation conditions:
        <m>np_0\ge 10</m> and <m>n(1-p_0)\ge 10</m>.
      </p>
    </assemblage>

    <p>
      Complete a one-proportion <m>z</m> test for each problem. Define the
      population proportion; state <m>H_0</m> and <m>H_a</m> and identify
      which contains the claim; state the significance level and test
      direction; check the normal-approximation conditions; calculate
      <m>\widehat{p}</m> and <m>z</m>; find the p-value or critical value(s);
      state your decision about <m>H_0</m>; and write a conclusion about
      the original claim in context.
    </p>
    <p>
      Assume random sampling and independent observations for these
      exercises. Show your work or R commands. Keep full precision during
      calculations; round test statistics and critical values to three
      decimal places and p-values to four decimal places.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <page>
    <!-- Source: Adapted from 1propHT.docx, Question 1. Hypothetical sample changed to n=250 and x=100; claim changed to equality; original significance level retained. -->
    <exercise xml:id="ex-quiz-one-proportion-ht-att-3-oxycontin-equality" workspace="2.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
        In a hypothetical follow-up study, 100 of the 250 patients treated
        with OxyContin (oxycodone) develop nausea. A researcher claims that
        30% of OxyContin users develop nausea. Test this claim using a
        significance level of <m>0.01</m>. Is there sufficient evidence to
        reject the claim?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </page>
  <page>
    <!-- Source: 1propHT.docx, Question 5; original data, claim, and significance level; question limited to the claimed difference from random guessing. -->
    <exercise xml:id="ex-quiz-one-proportion-ht-att-3-touch-therapists" workspace="5.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
        In an experiment, touch therapists tried to identify which of two
        hands had been randomly selected without seeing or touching the
        selected hand. They were correct in 136 of 298 trials. Test the
        claim that their probability of a correct identification differs
        from the 50% expected from random guessing. Use a significance
        level of <m>0.12</m>. Is there sufficient evidence to support the
        claim?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </page>
</worksheet>
